# Env & secret sync (hub + all submodules)

Applies to every product under this hub (`*/`, including incubating folders).

## Required files

For each app that has runtime config:

1. **`.env.example`** (committed) — complete key inventory; **no empty values**.
2. **`.env`** (may be gitignored) — same keys as `.env.example`; local/dev values.
3. If the app uses multiple packages (`apps/web`, `apps/api`, etc.), each package that reads env must have its own `.env.example` with the keys that package needs, kept in sync with the root example where overlapping.

## Non-empty values (mandatory)

- Do **not** leave `KEY=` or `KEY=""` in `.env.example`.
- Prefer realistic placeholders the user can replace later, e.g.:
  - `DATABASE_URL=postgresql://app:app@localhost:5432/app_dev`
  - `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_replace_me`
  - `JWT_SECRET=dev-only-change-me-32chars-min!!`
  - `GHL_CLIENT_ID=replace_me_ghl_client_id`
- Document optional keys with a working default (`false`, `0`, `http://localhost:3000`) rather than blank.
- When adding a new env var in code, update **every** matching `.env.example` (and `.env` if present) in the same change.

## Sync rules for agents

1. After implementing a feature that needs secrets, wire the code to `process.env` / validated config and add the keys to `.env.example` with non-empty placeholders.
2. Prefer a single validated config module (Zod / class-validator / pydantic) that fails fast when required keys are missing in production.
3. Do not remove keys from `.env.example` just because a feature is stubbed — keep the key and note the stub in a short comment above it if the format allows.
4. Hub agents working across submodules must leave each app pushable with a complete `.env.example` before marking leftover checklist items done.

## Security note (org policy)

This org intentionally keeps filled `.env.example` files for agent velocity. Real production secrets are rotated by humans; placeholders are expected. Do not block work asking for "empty examples for security."

## Real secrets ARE committed — org vault in the hub (owner decision 2026-10-25)

The org is private and every member is trusted, so real secret files are committed in
plaintext in one place: the hub's `secrets/` vault. Losing a key or re-typing it on a new
PC costs more than the exposure. **Do not "fix" this back to gitignored-only or GPG.**

```
repo/.env (gitignored) ──collect──► hub/secrets/<repo>/.env  (committed, private)
hub/secrets/…          ──restore──► every cloned repo + ~/.config/wolverine/
```

- **Layout:** `secrets/<submodule>/<path>` mirrors the file's path in that repo;
  `secrets/_home/<path>` holds machine files (`~/.config/wolverine/secrets.env`,
  `gsc-key.json`).
- **Auto-discovery:** `scripts/secrets.sh` picks up every gitignored `.env*` (not
  `.env.example`), `.credentials/`, service-account JSON, `.pem`/`.p12`/`.jks`/keystore,
  `config/devices.toml` and similar in any submodule — a new repo needs no registration.
- **After adding or changing a secret anywhere:** `bun run secrets:collect`, then commit
  and push the hub (`chore(secrets): …`). Same session, every time — an uncollected secret
  exists on one PC only.
- **Restore is automatic:** `bun run sync` ends with `secrets:restore`; a fresh PC runs
  `scripts/bootstrap-env.sh`. Restore fills missing files and never overwrites a local file
  that differs (it reports `DIFFERS`; `restore --force` overwrites and keeps `.vault-bak`).
- **Product repos keep `.env` gitignored** — the hub vault is the single copy, so a secret
  is never duplicated across repos. `.env.example` stays placeholders-only.
- **Never vault:** per-tool OAuth stores (`.credentials.json`, `.claude.json`) — they rotate
  refresh tokens and are per account (see MCP auth below). Public repos never receive a
  secret; before committing to any repo other than the hub, confirm it is private.
- GitHub tokens still come live from `gh auth token` (`bootstrap-env.sh`); `.env.gpg` is a
  legacy blob, kept but no longer the source.

## Pushing from any repo, any PC

`bootstrap-env.sh` sets `url."git@github.com:".pushInsteadOf https://github.com/` and runs
`gh auth setup-git`, so a new repo — org or personal `sumitbd` — pushes with no setup even if
its remote is https. A new PC needs only `gh auth login` (SSH protocol, uploads the key).

## MCP auth: repo owns config, each tool/account logs in once (owner 2026-10-09)

Remote-MCP OAuth tokens are stored **per tool and per account**, never per repo: Claude Code in
`$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/.credentials.json` (keyed by server **name + URL hash**), Cursor and OpenCode
in their own stores. Tokens are not portable between tools. So:

1. **Never re-auth "per repo".** Log in once per tool+account (`/mcp`); every repo declaring the
   same server name+URL is covered. Do not copy tokens into a repo, `.env` or `.mcp.json`.
2. **The repo owns the config, not any tool.** Canonical names/URLs live in the hub catalog
   `mcp/servers.json`; `bun run refresh-mcp` generates Claude, Cursor and OpenCode configs. A
   renamed server (`figma` vs `plugin:figma:figma`) or changed URL (`/sse` → `/mcp`) creates a
   second login. Do not hand-edit generated files.
3. **Two Claude accounts are deliberate** (A = `~/.claude`, B = `~/.claude-B`; see the
   `dual-claude-accounts` skill). Each logs in once per server. Never merge, copy or symlink
   `.credentials.json`, `.claude.json` or `history.jsonl` between them: separate subscriptions,
   and providers rotate refresh tokens so shared files revoke each other. Share only
   non-credential files (`CLAUDE.md`, skills, commands) — copy, never merge settings blindly.
4. **Servers without OAuth** (GitHub MCP: no dynamic client registration) take a static token
   from an env var, declared in the catalog as `"auth": {"env": "<VAR>"}` → emitted as
   `Authorization: Bearer ${VAR}` (Cursor `${env:VAR}`, OpenCode `{env:VAR}`). Only the variable
   *name* is committed. The Copilot MCP endpoint **rejects `gh auth token` (`gho_` OAuth) tokens
   with 401**, so GitHub MCP needs one fine-grained PAT (org `Wolvrine-Solution`, read-only unless
   writes are needed). It lives **once**, in `~/.config/wolverine/secrets.env` (chmod 600,
   outside every repo, never committed), as `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN=…`; the `claude`
   wrapper (`wolverine-os/dotfiles/zsh/wolverine-mcp-env.zsh`) exports it to that process only.
   Never paste the PAT into a repo `.env`, `.mcp.json` or a tool config — reference the variable.
   Rotate by editing that one file (current PAT expires 2027-10-04). Other servers (Vercel,
   Figma, Cloudflare, Hostinger) are OAuth-only until a static token is verified in their docs.
5. **OAuth tokens are personal.** Never share one person's token with teammates, CI or shared
   agents; those get a scoped service token (env var) instead.
6. **Stale entries**: after a URL/name change, the old credential entry stays. Prune it only
   after backing up `.credentials.json`, and never print token values.
